WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Imagine a city on Mars.

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.070
People working, learning, making discoveries—and
sharing them with

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.940
people far from home.

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.110
On the Moon, another community has its own local
network.

5
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.550
Across these worlds, communication follows
different rhythms.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.810
A researcher records a discovery for a colleague
on Earth.

7
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.250
His message is ready.

8
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.780
But a complete path may not be.

9
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.730
The faster route is blocked for now.

10
00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.530
But another route is available.

11
00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.870
It takes longer, yet it can already carry part
of the discovery.

12
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.410
Some pieces can travel now.

13
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.970
Others wait for the faster link,

14
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.180
making use of both opportunities.

15
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.320
When the faster link opens, other pieces follow
that route—and arrive

16
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.680
first, even though they left later.

17
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.410
At the destination, each piece finds its place.

18
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.220
Using both paths makes more capacity available.

19
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.630
The pieces need not arrive in the order they
were sent.

20
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.350
When no contact is available, information waits
within storage and

21
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.380
expiry limits.

22
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.520
PRP applications can retain messages before
transmission and send them

23
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.070
when contact returns.

24
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.130
Distance still takes time.

25
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.500
If a transmission is lost, a relay holding a
valid copy can try again.

26
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.600
Recovery need not start all the way back at the
source.

27
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:55.000
The source retains its copy until end-to-end
confirmation, expiry, or cancellation.

28
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.360
His colleague receives the discovery.

29
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.420
Life continues on Mars, on the Moon, and on
Earth. Different networks. Different moments.

30
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.310
A conversation that reaches beyond the line of
sight.

